stories  about Jack Daniel and and one that was  just an absolute classic was told by  this family about how Jack used to on  Sundays ride his horse out to the - at  one church or another and as the the  young ladies came out of the church you  know dressed in their best of course he  would pay the local boys nickels and  dimes to give him the lowdown on  particular women and and this is how I  started to find out that he was actually  quite a ladies man and he never married  another great source for starting to  piece together his life were newspaper  articles and again that can really be  you know just drudgery just grueling  work going through literally every  weekly issue of the Lynchburg Falcon and  other local newspapers but there was one  that I know there was a bunch that were  great that it came across but one in  particular from 1877 there was a major  piece on the local distillers and in  that I discovered why he calls his  whiskey old number seven what had  happened was the government you're in a  tax district and the government gave  each distiller a particular number to  identify that distillery in that  district and Jack was in district 4 and  in his number was number seven now he'd  had that for a couple of years but the  government decided in its wisdom to  combine combined district four with  district  now district 5 included some Nashville  area distilleries that were much larger  and much more sophisticated than these  fellows down in the Lynchburg area and  that did not leave them happy at all in  fact they sent a couple of their leaders  up to DC to complain so Jack what now  was in district 5 and out of all the  district 4 distilleries that were  combined into five he was the only one  who had his number changed  he became number 16 in district 5 now  that may not seem like a big deal but  back then you sold your whiskey in jugs  and in barrels and you didn't have a  brand name what you had on there was the  government stamp with your district and  your number and that's how your  customers knew you so Jack who made one  of the finer whiskeys decided to heck  with that I'm gonna put old number 7 on  on my my barrels and jugs from now on  and you'll see in the book I discussed  that it went went through he had a bunch  of different names you know old-time  distillery number seven Jack Daniels old  number seven so this was his way of not  just revolting against what the the  government the revenue department had  done but also to remind his customers  who he was so that's where the name  comes from so I started to piece  together as his life and was really able  to flesh it out now I want to give you a  thumbnail sketch of his rise from from  the backwoods to really the world stage  but before we do that we just have to  review a little bit of background on on  whiskey making and his family and his  roots whiskey of course originated in  Scotland or Ireland depending whether  you're talking to an Irishman or a  Scotsman they've thought about it for  centuries what we know though is that in  the 1400s they were making single malt  in Scotland now those whiskies are made  with barley in this country we don't  have barley  so when they started to migrate here the  predominant crop was corn there was a  Episcopalian missionary and you'll find  out that the clergy are always involved  in whiskey making it's great you know  they got to have all the spirits there  was a missionary in North Carolina who  figured out how to distill corn and and  make corn whiskey and he preferred it to  his English ale now unfortunately for  him he proceeded it to give it give it  to the Native Americans who killed  killed them on a drunken rampage and I'm  not sure if they were mad at him because  of the corn whiskey or mad at him  because he was a missionary you know  that's up for debate but - now corn we  have corn whiskey being made here right  before the Revolutionary War people from  Scotland Ireland the Scotch Irish that  came from Ulster started immigrating  here in droves they came through New  York Philadelphia they pushed West to  the Allegheny Mountains and then they  came down the Shenandoah Valley started  settling into Kentucky they also came  via South Carolina and North Carolina  Jack's grandfather came via New Bern  North Carolina right before the war he  fought in the Revolutionary War and  around 1800 moved inland to Tennessee  and then the Jack's father was born  shortly thereafter and it was Jack's  father who around 1835 migrated up to  the Lynchburg area and in the early  1800s that that was still wilderness  they were really pioneers that Daniels  family and a handful of others were and  still are considered the pioneers so  that's how they moved into the area and  what they brought with them of course is  the whiskey making tradition and that is  why Lynchburg and in certain areas in  Kentucky and North Carolina are just so  well-known for their whiskey making  because  of the contingent of scotch-irish  Irish and Scottish folks that that  became pioneers in those areas now as  far as Jax life goes I mentioned he was  born in 18-49 and he died in 1911 he is  and and this was something that  surprised me a classic rags to riches  story when he was born in 49 his mother  died shortly thereafter and his father  remarried but then during the Civil War  he lost his father and he despised his  stepmother so he's essentially an orphan  at that point and he ran away from home  and he had all of nine dollars to his  name now he was adopted by a local  family Dan call who you'll read about in  the legends of Jack Daniel and Dan call  is the one who taught him how to distill  and it was really Dan call who had a  slave nearest Green who then remained  with dan call after the war which shows  you what kind of man Dan call musta been  and it was nearest green who really  showed Jack all the tricks of the trade  because prior to the war it was the  slaves who really developed some of the  techniques that refined the whiskey now  coming out of the Civil War as you know  Middle Tennessee was completely  plundered you know that the Union troops  had come in here in sixty-two ravaged  the place it was their breadbasket for  the next three years so how did you get  on your feet  as a farmer or as a returning soldier  you made whiskey  it was the most efficient use of corn  crop it was also very easy to get to  market you know the corn could spoil on  the way to market but whiskey wasn't  going to spoil so there was a lot of  moonshine going on a lot of distilling  and in these areas and of course Jack  and Dan call we're moonshining in those  years right after the Civil War because  laws were not being enforced by the  federal government at that time and  there's great stories about Jack Daniel  going down to Huntsville you have you  have to read about him  you know he's only a teenager and and  his personal courage really starts to  show now as I mentioned 1866 is the date  the company says that that he started  his business but it's really 1875 so  Jack spent a lot of years learning the  trade and it was a slow process there  was one other thing going on in his life  after his father died the farm went up  for sale and it was sold in 1867 and the  proceeds were gonna be divided among ten  children Jack was one of 10 the last of  ten but the family started feuding over  the money because the older kids had  already been given gifts by from the  father like one had received $500 which  was a substantial sum then another had  received the prized horse so the younger  generation said no no now hold on a  minute  we want our fair share so it was  actually in the courts for a number of  years the family fought it out and the  judge decided that they had to pull  together all of the family's belongings  everything that had been given prior to  Calloway Daniels death and it and when  it was all pulled together it mounted to  a little over ten thousand dollars now  Jack who only had nine dollars to his  name coming into this realized the  thousand dollars plus as a windfall now  that was a huge sum in those days you  could buy a beautiful farm for three or  four hundred dollars the case was  settled in 1875 and that's when Jack  goes into business he buys himself one  hundred and forty acres spread he goes  into business with Dan call now Dan call  is a lay preacher in the local Lutheran  Church he only lasts a few years because  of pressure from his church and his wife  to drop out of the distilling business  and it's an 1884 Jack's now on his own  that he buys the legendary hollow witch  which of course you'll read about if you  go to Lynchburg and do the tour you'll  see the Hollow  okay so now he  is in business on his own and 20 years  later is when he hits the world stage  and that's at the 1904 st. Louis World's  Fair he enters the competition there for  liqueurs for whiskey and of course  everyone is expecting some fine single  malt scotch to take the prize or an  Irish whiskey to take the prize but Jack  wins the gold medal and and it makes big  news among the distilleries because you  know who is this guy well he's he was  known as the little man from Lynchburg  now here's the sad part about this story  in his local paper  he got one little line of recognition  and you know this was a big deal right  next to him you know I was reading the  paper right next to him there was an  article that I got a few inches of press  you know a lot more than you got about a  man living in Chicago who had filed a  petition in court to get an injunction  against his wife to stop her from  talking so here's this ridiculous  article in in the Fayetteville paper and  right next to this court Jack get in one  little line so you know what what is  happening that that just gives you sort  of a sketch of his rise but you know  what's happening at the time that that  that the newspaper would basically  disregard his finest accomplishment well  you have the dry forces the temperance  movement the people who want national  prohibition at work and and they are  coming to the fore at this point they  are they're pushing hard and they've got  a grip not only on politicians but on  the media and you see a polarization  going on in society like like we have  today you know if you look at gay  marriage there's that polarization it  was the same thing back then when you  read the newspaper articles in fact  there were a lot of Local Option laws  that would allow towns individually to  vote to go dry and what would happen is  that the the local preacher or minister  would actually stand at the ballot box  and if anyone voted not to go dry he  would shout out their name  so that was democracy at work in circa  1900 and one of the leaders of course it  was Carrie nation who made headline news  on both sides of the Atlantic and and  she was she was a piece of work and I  have to quote what she has to say about  men  she says that men are nicotine soaked  beer besmirched whiskey greased red-eyed  Devils and this was a woman who was  actually married twice and she had a  child so she was something now something  else I want to mention is Jack of course  was in the Bible Belt right he was  raised in the primitive Baptist Church  and so drinking was frowned upon and as  you read the book you'll see that  there's this constant tension between  the distillers and and of course the the  Baptist Church now coming out of the  Civil War and and through into the 1870s  distillers were very important to this  area because they hired a lot of people  and they brought in a lot of money and  they were community leaders by the time  he gets the 1890s things that  flip-flopped you know we have to talk a  little bit about Jack Daniels the  marketing genius because of course  that's what people think about today and  he he really did start it he really was  the the seed to the kind of promotion  you still see today if you go down to  Lynchburg you're gonna see the the  distillery's calendar with you know good  old distillery boys sitting on old  pickup trucks and and barrels John away  and and and brown-forman  who owns a distillery really has  promoted that kind of backwoods  down-to-earth image and but that's what  Jack was he was completely rooted in the  traditional way of doing things  you know this was a guy who was born in  Lynchburg became famous could have moved  into the glitzy Nats Nashville  and but he never did he really was  rooted in in the past now as far as his  marketing genius you have to picture  this guy he was five foot two as an  adult five foot seven was the average  male height at that time he had a  flowing mustache goatee he had his  signature outfit which you'll you'll see  plenty of photos of which was a  knee-length frock coat silk line vest a  broad tie a wide brim planters hat so  when this guy walked into a Nashville  saloon you didn't forget him  you just didn't forget him and he spent  a lot of time in saloons especially in  the men's quarter here so there was that  image of him that you just simply  couldn't forget now also he had his PT  Barnum side he sponsored balloon  launches which were a big deal then to  bring attention to himself and his  whiskey he formed that Jack Daniels old  number 7 cornet band which you'll see  photos of that's used in advertising for  the product today he even sent a barrel  of whiskey to Queen Victoria and Britain  to bring attention to himself and I like  to say that looking at photos of her  with her stiff upper lip I don't think  she ever had a drop unless it was for  medicinal purposes but so he really you  know he knew how to promote himself then  you have the square bottle from 1897  that's when he adopted the square bottle  and that was partly by luck a company  from Illinois was was promoting this  square bottle and prior to that time  bottles really weren't used there wasn't  the technology to make them on a mass  scale so this the salesman showed up and  Jack said that's the bottle for me that  square bottle will show people that I'm  a square dealer I'm a good guy because  of course distillers had had a shady  image so the company really took that  and ran with it and I should sort of  wrap up here with with what happened in  the last years of Jack's life  in 1907 he bequeaths the whole  distillery the whole shebang to his two  nephews one of them being LEM Motlow LEM  Motlow buys out the other nephew and  again if you look at the at the bottle  you'll see LEM Motlow proprietor on it  because it's LEM who carries the torch  through prohibition and through World  War two now Jack sells out in 1907  Tennessee goes effectively dry in 1909  ten years before national prohibition  it's a heartbreaker for Jack he dies in  1911 at a relatively young age and what  you'll find out in the book is that he  did not die from kicking us safe as  legend has it but I won't give away how  he did LEM Motlow dies in 1947 and his  four sons take over the business they  sell out to brown-forman  which is based in Louisville and it's  brown foreman who who really has the  money to put into marketing this product  and one of the first things they do is  create the Tennessee Squires do we have  any Squires here we have to have a few  of course they're everywhere and that's  you know it's a fan club for for Jack  Daniels drinkers but there's a great  story associated with it that that I'll  wrap up with here Boris Yeltsin the  President of Russia is he's on his  deathbed at least everyone thinks he is  you know half dozen years or so ago and  so that their their secret police are  going through his personal papers  because he's a shady guy and they just  want to make sure there's nothing in  there they have to worry about when  Boris dies that and that might become  public and what they find is a deed for  land in Tennessee so immediately the red  flags go up because you know it it's an  official deed right so red flags go up  they call the Russian embassy here in  the States that embassy in turn calls  down to the Czech Daniel distillery and  says what is going on here  and it turns out that that Boris was a  Tennessee Squire and the amazing thing  is I always figured him for a vodka  drinker but that just shows you the kind  of mystique Jack Daniels has now I'd  happy to be happy to answer any  questions you have about the research  and Jack Daniel the company today how  they make their whisky whatever you  would like yes you mentioned that a  whiskey was made out of corn which of  course is the basis for bourbon but Jack  Daniels this difference that it is  filtered through charcoal right gives  you the particular flavor can you  comment on that sure a hundred years ago  Jack Daniels was a bourbon okay same  tradition 50 had to be 51% corn had to  be aged in charred white oak barrels now  the difference is and this is called the  Lincoln County process that's the  filtering through the pulverized  charcoal that's made from the sugar  maple tree now that Jack Daniel  distillery to say it decided that  whatever reason they had a finer product  because of that filtering and they  wanted to be recognized as something  other than bourbon  so they actually applied to the federal  government to be recognized as Tennessee  whiskey as opposed to bourbon so so  that's what happened that that's how  that occurred but you know bourbon every  everyone was making bourbon then yes I  was just devastated when you said that  he didn't die from kicking that safe  I have heard Sammy gulley talk about on  the Jack Daniels tour more times than I  can count and he always says that he got  gangrene in his big toe from kicking  that safe and so  did he die from gangrene he did not from  kicking the safe he kicked that safe in  either like 1905 1906 according to the  legend now you know he didn't die until  1911 that's a long time for the gangrene  to work its way up from his big toe I'm  not gonna I'm not gonna depress you with  with how he got it but it's in there  it's in the book yes yes you had said  that he was born in 18-49 and and  everyone I mean all the things say he  was born in 1850 what is the well in  eighteen 1850s in a nice round number  you know but what what I found was his  sister's diary and and back then in  Diaries you recorded when people were  born when they died and the weather and  everything else like that that would  appear to be mundane and his sister  records exactly when their mother died  and it was in January don't quote me on  this because I don't quite remember the  date but it was in 49 and then so  obviously he had to have been born prior  to that and then when you go to the  census records he is a year old in 1850  and local folks went around doing you  know canvassing the homes to put  together these census so you have to  pretty much assume that they knew the  Daniel family and knew when he was born  other questions yes  did he develop the sour mash process and  and did he start with that sour mash  process in his business he didn't  develop it that was already being used  at the time and that this outlet for  those of you don't know the sour mash  process is when you if you use some of  the leftover beer which is what what  you're making when when you create the  mash before you distill it use some that  leftover and put it into the next batch  to create it like a continuity and it  gives you a slightly smoother whiskey  versus a sweet so that was part of the  Lincoln County process and then that you  know it been a tradition for a while  other questions  I'm trying to get some idea of how big a  businessman he was what was the most  number of people that he had working for  him in in so far as you've been able to  find out probably a dozen at the most it  did not take a lot of people to run a  distillery and he capped off his  production of 300 gallons a day because  he wanted to stay a fine whiskey he  wanted to be known as making it in  batches as opposed to a big outfit so  that was all he really needed and you  know if you go to a distillery today  that you don't need a whole lot of  people to run it I will say it was a  very savvy businessman Dan call whom he  lived with for a while  dan calls uncle actually invested in  stocks and was very sophisticated and  businessman himself so Jack's business  education was excellent  you know homeschooled but he had a very  good picture of the business world  well mostly grass roots you also  advertised in newspapers and back then  you know people bought it in bulk the  grocers bought it in bulk the saloons  bought it in bulk and then they put it  into their own bottles or jugs so you  really were more of a wholesaler as  opposed to you know trying to attract an  individual customer and it's not till  the late 1890s when bottles come into  play in the bottle and bond act which  you'll read about in the book where the  distillers are now putting their own  labels on on the bottle with slogans and  and pictures and and that sort of thing  so that that was really towards the  twilight of his career when that started  to happen was the cave spring that's  used now used in the earlier processing  at the distillery well beginning in 1884  it was prior to that he was on what's  known as still house Creek out by the  Dan Kahl farm  anybody else questions okay well I  really appreciate your time and it's  been a pleasure
 

